; ANSWER_DIVERGES_BY_VANTAGE_POINT — the stale side.
;
; The counterpart to zones/unsigned/split.test.zone: the same name, the same
; delegation, a different SPF record. This side does not authorise the platform,
; so a check run against dns-divergent fails the SPF requirement while the same
; check against dns-auth passes it.
;
; Nobody is lying here. This is what mid-propagation looks like, and it is why a
; single disagreeing vantage point must produce uncertainty rather than a
; failure — firing `domain.failed` off this would page a customer's customer
; for a zone that is in the middle of being updated correctly.
;
; Keep the NS RRset in step with the honest side. If the two disagree about the
; delegation itself this stops testing divergent answers and starts testing a
; broken delegation.
$ORIGIN split.test.
$TTL 300

@       IN SOA  ns1.test. hostmaster.propgate.invalid. (
                1 7200 3600 1209600 300 )
@       IN NS   ns1.test.
@       IN NS   ns-divergent.test.

; The platform is absent from this side's SPF. Same name, different answer.
@                   IN TXT  "v=spf1 include:two.spf.test -all"
